 "Is it a red flag if my 4-year-old cannot read yet?": 1,
 "No—most children begin decoding around age 5–6. What matters more at age 4 is exposure to books, rhymes, and conversation. Mention persistent language delays to your paediatrician.": 1,
 "Does starting to read earlier give a lasting academic advantage?": 1,
 "Research does not consistently show long-term benefits from pushing formal reading before a child is developmentally ready; quality of early language environment and love of books are stronger predictors of later literacy.": 1,
 "How can I support reading readiness before school?": 1,
 "Read aloud daily from birth, discuss stories, sing nursery rhymes, point out letters in everyday environments, and visit the library often—these activities build vocabulary, phonological awareness, and print concepts.": 1,
 "How to choose: what age do kids start reading": 1,
 "Birth–age 3: Children build the oral-language and print-awareness foundations of reading through being read to, talked to, and sung to (AAP Books Build Connections Toolkit).": 1,
 "Ages 3–4: Most recognise some letters, especially those in their own name; they 'read' books from memory and pictures (CDC milestones, 4-year checklist).": 1,
 "Ages 4–5: Letter-name knowledge, rhyme sensitivity, and some letter-sound connections emerge; CDC notes naming a few letters by age 4 as a typical milestone.": 1,
